  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-13",
    "title": "NGC 206 and the Star Clouds of Andromeda",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  The large stellar association cataloged as  NGC 206  is nestled within the dusty arms of the neighboring  Andromeda galaxy  along with the galaxy's pinkish star-forming regions. Also known as M31,  the spiral galaxy is a mere  2.5 million light-years away. NGC 206 is found at the center of  this sharp and detailed close-up  of the southwestern  extent  of Andromeda's disk. The bright, blue  stars of NGC 206  indicate its youth. In fact, its youngest massive stars are less than 10 million years old. Much larger than the open or galactic clusters of young stars  in the disk   of our Milky Way galaxy,   NGC 206  spans about 4,000 light-years. That's comparable in size to the giant stellar nurseries  NGC 604  in nearby spiral M33 and the  Tarantula Nebula  in the Large Magellanic Cloud.   Tomorrow's picture:  pixels in space",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Andromeda_closeupCruz4096.jpg",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-13-ngc-206-and-the-star-clouds-of-andromeda/",
    "credit": " David Cruz ",
    "alt": "Close up of NGC 206 in the Andromeda galaxy.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-13T09:03:59.502211+00:00"
